from lib import *
C=10;S='social-science';CH='history-world-war-ii'
def rows(title,data,rh=34):
    t=f'<text x="280" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">{title}</text>'
    for i,(c,a,b) in enumerate(data):
        t+=f'<rect x="15" y="{36+i*rh}" width="530" height="{rh-5}" rx="6" fill="{c}" stroke="#333"/><text x="23" y="{56+i*rh}" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">{a}</text><text x="140" y="{56+i*rh}" font-size="12">{b}</text>'
    return svg(560,46+len(data)*rh,t)
# ---------- A: causes and outbreak ----------
lesson(C,S,CH,'causes-course-and-results',"World War II: causes and outbreak","Money by the wheelbarrow",
"In the early 1920s a worker in Germany could be paid in the morning with a wheelbarrow full of notes, and by evening the notes bought less bread. People carried money in wheelbarrows. That anger, and the leaders who fed on it, is where World War II begins.",
["World War I was fought from 1914 to 1918 and World War II from 1939 to 1945, and both are called world wars because the fighting covered huge areas and killed very many civilians as well as soldiers. @ 1914-18 and 1939-45",
 "In both wars Britain, France, Russia and the United States fought against an alliance led by Germany, and in the second war Germany's allies were Italy and Japan. @ Allies and Axis",
 "The Treaty of Versailles, signed in June 1919, angered Germans with three clauses: they gave up land to the west, north and east, they had to disarm to a very small force, and they had to pay for the cost of the war. @ Versailles 1919",
 "The immediate and main cause of the war was the aggressive military drive of a resurgent Germany and a fast-growing Japan. @ main cause",
 "The problems of the post-war years led to extreme right-wing dictatorships in Italy under Mussolini, in Germany under Hitler and in Spain under Franco. @ dictators",
 "Germany suffered high unemployment and terrible inflation, its currency became almost worthless, and people blamed the reparations, although the demands were later reduced after several rounds of talks. @ inflation",
 "Adolf Hitler used this anger: a gifted speaker, he founded the National Socialist party, the Nazis, and built support on the idea that Germans were a pure Aryan race and on deep hatred of Jews. @ Nazis",
 "Hitler came to power in 1933 and ruled until 1945. @ 1933",
 "Against the Treaty, Hitler began to rearm Germany, and the arms factories and army recruitment, paid by heavy government spending, revived the economy and removed unemployment. @ rearmament",
 "The League of Nations was set up by the Treaty on the initiative of President Woodrow Wilson to mediate between countries and act against aggression, but the United States stayed out and the other Allies avoided intervening, so the League stayed ineffective. @ League of Nations",
 "Italy invaded Ethiopia in 1935 and Albania in 1939, and when Emperor Haile Selassie appealed to the League he got no help. @ Ethiopia 1935",
 "Japan invaded Manchuria in 1931 and China in 1937, seizing Beijing, and the Allies and the League did nothing. @ Manchuria 1931",
 "After Mussolini's invasion of Ethiopia broke Italy's ties with Britain and France, Italy drew closer to Germany, and the Rome-Berlin Axis appeared in 1936, later widening into the Rome-Berlin-Tokyo Axis when Japan joined. @ Axis 1936",
 "In 1938 Hitler took Austria and Czechoslovakia, claiming that the German-speaking people of the Sudetenland should join one nation. @ Sudetenland",
 "Britain and France stayed non-interventionist under Prime Ministers Baldwin and Chamberlain, and the United States, busy recovering from the Great Depression, was indifferent to the outside world. @ non-intervention",
 "In 1938 Chamberlain signed the Munich Pact, a shameful acceptance of the German takeover of the Sudetenland, and in 1939 the Soviet Union signed its own non-aggression pact with Germany, since the Western powers and the Soviets distrusted each other. @ Munich Pact",
 "Hitler broke his Munich promise at once, invaded Czechoslovakia in 1939 and then attacked Poland, and Britain and France declared war on Germany in September 1939. @ September 1939",
 "Italy joined Germany in June 1940 and Japan joined in September 1940, completing the Axis powers. @ Axis powers"],
("Which three clauses of the Treaty of Versailles angered Germany, and how did the anger help Hitler?",[("Germany lost land in the west, north and east, had to disarm, and had to pay reparations for the war.","These were the three clauses the Germans resented most."),("Inflation and unemployment made the reparations feel unbearable, so people turned to a leader who promised a glorious past.","Hitler used oratory and the idea of racial superiority to win support."),("Hitler came to power in 1933 and began to rearm in breach of the Treaty.","The rearmament revived the economy, which made him popular.")]),
("Explain why the League of Nations failed to stop the aggression of the 1930s.",["The United States did not join it","Other Allies stayed non-interventionist","It could not act against Italy over Ethiopia or Japan over Manchuria"],["Thinking the League had its own army","Thinking the League stopped Germany in 1936"]),
["Versailles (1919): lost land, disarmed, reparations.","Hitler in power in 1933; dictators in Italy, Germany, Spain.","League of Nations failed: no US, no action on Ethiopia and Manchuria.","Axis 1936; Munich Pact 1938; war declared September 1939."],
["n|Year the Treaty of Versailles was signed|1919|It was signed in June 1919.",
 "n|Year Hitler came to power in Germany|1933|He ruled until 1945.",
 "m|The League of Nations was set up on the initiative of|Woodrow Wilson|Winston Churchill~He led Britain later;Neville Chamberlain~He signed the Munich Pact;Franklin Roosevelt~He started Lend Lease|It was President Wilson of the United States.",
 "m|Which major power never joined the League of Nations?|United States|Britain~Britain was a member;France~France was a member;Italy~Italy was a member at first|The popular mood favoured isolation.",
 "t|Italy invaded Ethiopia in 1935.|True|Emperor Haile Selassie appealed to the League in vain.",
 "t|The Munich Pact was signed between Hitler and Stalin.|False|It was concluded by Chamberlain with Germany in 1938.",
 "m|The dictator who ruled Spain in this period was|Franco|Mussolini~He ruled Italy;Hitler~He ruled Germany;Stalin~He ruled the Soviet Union|The three dictators were Mussolini, Hitler and Franco.",
 "m|The Sudetenland, claimed by Hitler, was part of|Czechoslovakia|Austria~Hitler took Austria separately;Poland~Poland was attacked next;Hungary~It was not the target|Its people spoke German.",
 "s|The 1936 alliance between Italy and Germany was called the Rome-Berlin ____.|Axis|It later became the Rome-Berlin-Tokyo Axis.",
 "s|The emperor of Ethiopia who appealed to the League of Nations was Haile ____.|Selassie|He got no help."],
tname="Causes and outbreak of World War II")
# ---------- B: course in Europe and Africa ----------
b_svg=rows("World War II in Europe and Africa",[('#d6eaf8','Sept 1939','Britain and France declare war on Germany'),('#d5f5e3','May 1940','Dunkirk: 300,000+ soldiers on the beaches'),('#fcf3cf','Jul-Oct 1940','Battle of Britain and the Blitz'),('#fadbd8','Mar 1941','Lend Lease begins'),('#ebdef0','Jun 1941','Germany invades Russia'),('#fdebd0','1942','El Alamein and Stalingrad'),('#d6eaf8','1943','Italy surrenders'),('#d5f5e3','May 1945','War in Europe ends')])
lesson(C,S,CH,'course-of-the-war-in-europe-and-north-africa',"The war in Europe and North Africa","Thirty miles of a river city",
"Imagine a city 30 miles long along a great river, making tractors and guns, and bearing the name of the enemy leader. Hitler wanted it badly, and the fight for it became a turning point of the war.",
["World War II was fought on two fronts, Europe and the Asia Pacific, with the Allies against Germany and Italy in Europe and against Japan in the Asia Pacific. @ two fronts",
 "It was a modern war of tanks, submarines, battleships, aircraft carriers, fighters and bombers, so it needed raw materials, factories and technical improvements, and it became an expensive, long war of attrition. @ war of attrition",
 "Germany used Blitzkrieg, the lightning strike, to storm into countries, taking Denmark, Norway and then France, until by 1941 all mainland Europe up to the Russian frontier was in Axis hands. @ Blitzkrieg",
 "At Dunkirk in May 1940 more than 300,000 British and French soldiers were forced back to the beaches, and Britain would have struggled to regroup if so many had been lost. @ Dunkirk 1940",
 "In the Battle of Britain, from July 1940, Hitler tried to force Britain to accept peace by bombing ports, airfields and factories, and in September 1940 London suffered the Blitz. @ Battle of Britain",
 "The air campaign failed because radar let the Royal Air Force spot German bombers early and inflict severe losses, so the raids stopped after October 1940 and the invasion plan was dropped. @ radar",
 "President Roosevelt could not take the United States into the war directly, so in March 1941 he began Lend Lease, sending arms, food and supplies to Britain disguised as a loan, worth 46.5 billion dollars between 1941 and 1945. @ Lend Lease",
 "In June 1941 Germany invaded Russia to seize land for Germans, destroy the communist regime and take resources such as oil, and its army pushed 1000 miles in and marched on Moscow. @ Russia 1941",
 "Soviet resistance and the fierce Russian winter finally stopped the German advance. @ Russian winter",
 "Stalingrad, an industrial city of about 30 miles along the Volga, mattered because taking it would cut Soviet transport to the south, open the Caucasus oil fields and give Hitler a propaganda victory, and Germany's plan was called Fall Blau, Operation Blue. @ Stalingrad",
 "The Battle of Stalingrad ran from 17 July 1942 to 2 February 1943, and the Soviet people defended the city even after about 15 million civilian deaths and about 10 million soldiers killed, over a tenth of Russia's population. @ 1942-43",
 "In North Africa General Rommel's forces took ground quickly and left the British only Egypt, but at El Alamein in 1942 the Allies under General Montgomery defeated the Germans and Italians and chased them out. @ El Alamein",
 "The victory in North Africa gave the Allies a base to invade Italy, and in 1943 a new Italian government surrendered after Mussolini was thrown out. @ Italy 1943",
 "The Germans set Mussolini up as a puppet ruler in the north, and Italian partisans killed him in April 1945. @ Mussolini",
 "General Eisenhower led the Allied invasion of Normandy in France, and the German army was slowly pushed back. @ Normandy",
 "From 1944 the Russian army attacked from the east, capturing much of Eastern Europe and Poland, and in 1945 it occupied parts of Berlin. @ Berlin 1945",
 "Hitler committed suicide in April 1945, the war in Europe ended in May 1945, and Germany was divided into two sections. @ May 1945"],
("Why did Germany want Stalingrad, and why did it fail to take it?",[("Stalingrad was an industrial city on the Volga making armaments and tractors.","Capturing it would cut Soviet links to the south."),("It would also open the Caucasus oil fields and give Hitler a propaganda victory over Stalin's name.","Fall Blau, Operation Blue, began on 28 June 1942 with German successes."),("The Soviet people defended it fiercely, and the Russian winter helped, so the battle ended on 2 February 1943.","The Germans were forced back.")]),
("Describe how radar and Lend Lease helped Britain survive.",["Radar allowed the RAF to inflict severe losses on German bombers","The Battle of Britain raids stopped after October 1940","Lend Lease sent arms and food to Britain as a loan worth 46.5 billion dollars"],["Thinking Lend Lease was a gift with no return","Thinking the United States entered the war in March 1941"]),
["Blitzkrieg conquered most of Europe by 1941.","Dunkirk, Battle of Britain (radar), Lend Lease.","Germany invaded Russia in 1941; Stalingrad 1942-43.","El Alamein, Italy's surrender 1943, Normandy; Europe's war ended May 1945."],
["n|Between 1941 and 1945 Lend Lease was worth about how many billion dollars|46.5|The total aid was 46.5 billion dollars.",
 "n|More than how many thousand British and French soldiers were forced back to Dunkirk|300|More than 300,000.",
 "m|Which device helped the RAF win the Battle of Britain?|Radar|Sonar~It detects underwater objects;Telegraph~Too slow for aircraft;Searchlight~Not the new secret device|Radar detected aircraft at a distance.",
 "m|The Allied general who defeated the Germans at El Alamein was|Montgomery|Eisenhower~He led the Normandy invasion;Rommel~He led the Germans;Chiang Kai-shek~He led China|It was a victory in North Africa in 1942.",
 "t|The German invasion of Britain succeeded in 1940.|False|The air campaign failed and the invasion plan was dropped.",
 "t|Lend Lease supplies to Britain were disguised as a loan.|True|Roosevelt could not intervene directly.",
 "m|Stalingrad stood on the banks of the river|Volga|Danube~It flows through central Europe;Rhine~It flows through Germany;Thames~It flows through London|It lay along the Volga.",
 "m|The German tactic of the lightning strike was called|Blitzkrieg|Lend Lease~An American supply programme;Fall Blau~A plan for Stalingrad;Panchsheel~An Indian policy|It overran Denmark, Norway and France.",
 "s|The German plan to capture Stalingrad was called Fall ____ (Operation Blue).|Blau|Blau is German for blue.",
 "s|The Allied invasion of France in 1944 landed in ____.|Normandy|It was led by General Eisenhower."],
svg=(b_svg,"A timeline of World War II in Europe and Africa from 1939 to 1945","The war in Europe and North Africa"),
tname="The war in Europe and North Africa")
# ---------- C: Asia-Pacific and end of the war ----------
lesson(C,S,CH,'war-in-the-asia-pacific-and-the-end-of-the-war',"The Asia-Pacific war and its end","A surprise at dawn in Hawaii",
"On a quiet morning in December 1941, American ships lay at anchor in Hawaii. Japan struck without warning, and the war in Asia and the war in Europe suddenly became one.",
["Japan dreamed of a glorious empire in the same way as Hitler, and it invaded Manchuria in 1931 even though China appealed to the League of Nations. @ Manchuria 1931",
 "In 1937 Japan invaded China, seized Beijing and the Shanghai region, and captured the capital Nanjing at the end of the year, where civilians were killed in huge numbers. @ China 1937",
 "Guangzhou and many other places were overrun, and the Chinese army under Chiang Kai-shek retreated to the hills in the west and kept on fighting. @ Chiang Kai-shek",
 "In December 1941 Japan attacked American naval bases at Pearl Harbour in Hawaii without warning, hoping to cripple the Pacific fleet so that it could advance into Southeast Asia unopposed. @ Pearl Harbour",
 "Many battleships and fighter planes were destroyed, the United States declared war on Japan, and Britain and China joined in. @ US declares war",
 "This joined the Asia Pacific war and the European war into one cause and brought the United States, with its huge resources, into the Allies. @ one war",
 "Japan then swept through Southeast Asia, where Guam, the Philippines, Hong Kong, Singapore, Malaya, the Dutch East Indies, now Indonesia, and Burma fell. @ Southeast Asia",
 "In 1942 the American navy defeated the Japanese navy in the Battle of Midway, which turned the tide in favour of the Allies. @ Midway 1942",
 "The Battle of Guadalcanal in the Solomon Islands was a joint army and navy offensive lasting several months, and it was another crushing defeat for Japan. @ Guadalcanal",
 "The Americans retook the Philippines, and the Japanese were gradually thrown out of most of their conquests. @ Philippines",
 "In 1944 British and Indian armies pushed back the Japanese who tried to invade north-east India, and together with the Chinese they drove them out of Burma and freed Malaya and Singapore. @ Burma 1944",
 "The United States built an atomic bomb in a top secret project using the latest science, far more powerful than ordinary explosives. @ atom bomb",
 "Japan's generals refused to surrender, so the United States dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima, and when they still refused, another on Nagasaki in August 1945. @ Hiroshima, Nagasaki",
 "Japan announced its surrender on 15 August 1945 and signed it formally on 2 September 1945, ending World War II. @ 2 Sept 1945",
 "The war split the world into two blocs, one led by the United States against Communism and the other by Soviet Russia, and Europe was divided into Communist and non-Communist parts. @ two blocs",
 "The United States and the Soviet Union raced to build nuclear weapons, and defence spending in many countries shot up. @ nuclear race",
 "Colonial powers were forced to free their colonies in decolonization, and India was the first to become independent. @ decolonization"],
("Why was Pearl Harbour such a turning point of the war?",[("Japan attacked American naval bases in Hawaii in December 1941 without warning.","It wanted to cripple the Pacific fleet before attacking Southeast Asia."),("The United States declared war on Japan, and Britain and China joined.","The Asia Pacific and European wars became one."),("The United States, with huge resources, joined the Allies.","This shifted the balance towards the Allies.")]),
("Describe how the war in Asia ended.",["Midway and Guadalcanal defeated Japan","Hiroshima and Nagasaki were bombed in August 1945","Japan announced surrender on 15 August and signed on 2 September 1945"],["Saying Japan signed the surrender on 15 August","Thinking Britain dropped the atom bomb"]),
["Japan: Manchuria 1931, China 1937, Southeast Asia 1941-42.","Pearl Harbour brought the US in; Midway turned the tide.","Hiroshima and Nagasaki; surrender signed on 2 September 1945.","Result: two blocs, nuclear race, decolonization."],
["n|Year of the attack on Pearl Harbour|1941|It happened in December 1941.",
 "n|Year Japan invaded Manchuria|1931|China appealed to the League in vain.",
 "m|The battle that turned the tide against Japan was|Midway|El Alamein~It was in North Africa;Stalingrad~It was in Russia;Dunkirk~It was in France|The US navy defeated the Japanese navy there in 1942.",
 "m|The Chinese army in the war was led by|Chiang Kai-shek|Mao Zedong~Not named as the leader in this lesson;Eisenhower~An American general;Montgomery~A British general|He retreated west and kept fighting.",
 "t|Japan formally signed its surrender on 15 August 1945.|False|It announced surrender on 15 August and signed on 2 September 1945.",
 "t|British and Indian armies pushed back the Japanese from north-east India in 1944.|True|They also helped liberate Burma, Malaya and Singapore.",
 "m|The first Japanese city hit by an atomic bomb was|Hiroshima|Nagasaki~It was bombed afterwards;Tokyo~It was not an atomic target;Nanjing~It is in China|Nagasaki was bombed next.",
 "m|The Japanese army captured the Chinese capital at the end of 1937, which was|Nanjing|Beijing~It had been seized earlier;Shanghai~Its region was captured;Guangzhou~It was overrun later|Nanjing was the capital then.",
 "s|The Dutch East Indies is today called ____.|Indonesia|It fell to the Japanese in 1942.",
 "s|Peking, the old capital of China, is now written ____.|Beijing|Japan seized it in 1937."],
tname="The Asia-Pacific war and its end")
# ---------- D: Holocaust and new international order ----------
lesson(C,S,CH,'holocaust-and-the-new-international-order',"The Holocaust and the new international order","From a terrible war to a promise",
"In 1945 the world looked at the ruins and asked how such horror could be prevented again. The answer was a promise written on paper by 51 nations: the Charter of the United Nations.",
["After Hitler came to power the Jews were persecuted, deprived of their civil rights, robbed of their property and confined to ghettos. @ persecution",
 "The Nazis then planned the Final Solution, which meant destroying the Jews completely. @ Final Solution",
 "The word holocaust describes the killing of nearly six million Jews by the Germans during World War II. @ six million",
 "Hitler played on the anti-Jewish feeling, anti-Semitism, which was common in Germany and across Europe. @ anti-Semitism",
 "Jews were scattered across Europe and many were prominent in business, the performing arts and the professions, and the fact that money-lending was a major Jewish business deepened prejudice. @ prejudice",
 "By 1941 the United States and Britain were thinking seriously about international cooperation for lasting peace and for economic and financial stability. @ cooperation 1941",
 "The first step was the Atlantic Charter of 1941, a joint declaration by the United States and Britain, which all 26 countries fighting the Axis accepted on New Year's Day 1942. @ Atlantic Charter",
 "The Charter of the United Nations was signed on 26 June 1945 by 51 nations, and India, not yet independent, was also a signatory. @ 26 June 1945",
 "Today the United Nations has 193 member states and each, big or small, has an equal vote. @ 193 members",
 "The preamble of the Charter speaks of saving later generations from the scourge of war and of faith in human rights and the equal rights of men and women and of large and small nations. @ preamble",
 "In the aftermath of the Holocaust the Charter pledged respect for human rights for all without distinction of race, sex, language or religion, and a Commission on Human Rights was formed. @ human rights",
 "A committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, widow of President Franklin Roosevelt, with members such as Charles Malik, P.C. Chang, John Humphrey and Rene Cassin, drafted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. @ Eleanor Roosevelt",
 "The Declaration sets out basic rights in 30 articles, was adopted on 10 December 1948, and that day is observed as Human Rights Day. @ 10 December 1948",
 "About ninety national constitutions since 1948 contain provisions that can be traced to this Declaration. @ constitutions",
 "A major outcome of the Holocaust was the creation of the state of Israel as a homeland for the Jews. @ Israel",
 "The World Bank and the International Monetary Fund were also created as forums for countries large and small. @ World Bank, IMF"],
("Explain how the Holocaust influenced the creation of human rights law.",[("The Nazis killed nearly six million Jews in the Holocaust.","The world was shocked by the scale of the crime."),("The UN Charter pledged respect for human rights without distinction of race, sex, language or religion.","The UN formed a Commission on Human Rights."),("Its committee, chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, produced the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted on 10 December 1948.","It has 30 articles and inspired about ninety constitutions.")]),
("Trace the steps from the Atlantic Charter to the United Nations Charter.",["Atlantic Charter in 1941 by the United States and Britain","26 countries accepted the Declaration of the United Nations on 1 January 1942","The UN Charter was signed on 26 June 1945 by 51 nations"],["Thinking the UN Charter was signed in 1942","Thinking India could not sign because it was not free"]),
["Holocaust: Final Solution, nearly six million Jews killed.","UN: Atlantic Charter 1941, Charter signed 26 June 1945 by 51 nations; now 193 members.","UDHR: 30 articles, 10 December 1948, Eleanor Roosevelt's committee.","Israel created; World Bank and IMF founded."],
["n|Approximate number of Jews killed in the Holocaust, in millions|6|Nearly six million.",
 "n|Number of articles in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights|30|It was adopted in 1948.",
 "m|The committee that drafted the Universal Declaration was chaired by|Eleanor Roosevelt|Woodrow Wilson~He started the League;Rene Cassin~He was a member;Charles Malik~He was a member|She was the wife of Franklin Roosevelt.",
 "m|Human Rights Day is observed on|10 December|26 June~The day the UN Charter was signed;15 August~Japan's surrender announcement;1 January~The 1942 Declaration of the United Nations|The Declaration was adopted on 10 December 1948.",
 "t|India signed the UN Charter in 1945 although it was not independent then.|True|It was a signatory.",
 "t|The Charter of the United Nations was signed by 26 nations in 1945.|False|It was signed by 51 nations, and 26 accepted the 1942 Declaration.",
 "m|The United Nations now has how many member states?|193|51~The founding number;26~The 1942 Declaration;30~The articles in the Universal Declaration|Each has an equal vote.",
 "m|The Final Solution of the Nazis meant|Destroying the Jews completely|Sending Jews to Israel~It was not the plan;Giving Jews equal rights~The opposite;Ending the war~It was not about the war|It led to the Holocaust.",
 "s|The UN Charter was signed on 26 ____ 1945.|June|51 nations signed it.",
 "s|Prejudice against Jews is called anti-____.|Semitism|Hitler played on it."],
tname="The Holocaust and the new international order")
print('ok')
